\textbf{Cost Calculation:} At GPT-4 rates (\$2.50/M input, \$10/M output), with actual input/output token splits from logs: total cost = \textbf{\$247} for 285 edges (\textbf{\$0.87/edge avg}). Avg tokens/edge = 247K. This high per-edge cost---driven by multi-agent orchestration (avg 40 API calls/edge), iterative search, and full-text retrieval---motivates the single-run design.

\textbf{Comparison:} Deep Research is $\sim$119$\times$ more expensive per edge than citation judging (\$0.0073/edge with GPT-5-mini) and $\sim$512$\times$ more expensive than correctness judging (\$0.0017/edge).

\textbf{True Negative Coverage:} DR was only run on TP, FP, and FN edges (n=285, 20.4\% of edge space). True Negatives---the 1,109 edges neither LLM-generated nor expert-included---were \textbf{not} analyzed due to cost. Running DR on all TNs would cost an additional $\sim$\$269 and could reveal literature-supported relationships that \textit{both} experts and LLM missed.

\textbf{Reproduction:} \texttt{python final\_runs/cost\_analysis/compute\_dr\_costs.py}

\subsection{Limitations}

\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{No replication:} Single run per edge prevents uncertainty quantification and statistical testing of detection rates
    \item \textbf{Correlational confound:} Literature may contain correlational studies for plausible-but-incorrect relationships, confounding causal validation
    \item \textbf{Full-text access:} Paywalled articles may limit evidence quality; only abstracts and snippets available for many sources
    \item \textbf{Search API dependency:} Results depend on Brave Search's indexing; specialized databases (PubMed full-text) not directly queried
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Exploratory Analysis: Ground Truth Enrichment}
\label{sec:gt_enrichment_method}

Beyond detection rate analysis, we explore whether Deep Research results could \textit{enrich} the expert-constructed ground truth. The hypothesis is that some FP edges (``hallucinations'') represent valid causal relationships that experts omitted due to time constraints, scope decisions, or disciplinary blind spots---not because they lack scientific support.

We evaluate two enrichment scenarios:

\textbf{Scenario 1: Enriched Ground Truth (FP Promotion)}
\begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Identify DR-validated FPs:} FP edges where \texttt{direct\_causal\_found = True}
    \item \textbf{Promote to TP:} Reclassify these edges as True Positives in an ``enriched ground truth''
    \item \textbf{Recompute metrics:} Calculate Precision, Recall, and F1 against the enriched GT
\end{enumerate}

\textit{Question:} ``What if experts missed valid literature-supported relationships?''

\textbf{Scenario 2: LLM+DR System (FP Promotion + FN Discovery)}
\begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Start with Scenario 1:} Enriched GT with promoted FPs
    \item \textbf{Identify DR-validated FNs:} FN edges where \texttt{direct\_causal\_found = True}
    \item \textbf{Add to system output:} In a complete LLM+DR pipeline, DR would search comprehensively and discover these literature-supported edges the LLM missed
    \item \textbf{Recompute metrics:} TP includes discovered FNs; FN decreases accordingly
\end{enumerate}

\textit{Question:} ``What could a complete LLM+DR system achieve if DR both validated LLM output AND discovered additional literature-supported edges?''

\textbf{Rationale:} Scenario 1 tests whether expert GT underestimates LLM quality. Scenario 2 tests the theoretical ceiling of an integrated LLM+DR pipeline where literature search augments generation.

\textbf{Critical Caveats (see Discussion for details):}
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Domain variation:} FP promotion rates vary substantially across CLDs (28\% Depressive vs 76\% Older Persons), suggesting inconsistent enrichment
    \item \textbf{Correlational confound:} DR may validate correlational rather than causal evidence, inflating FP promotion
    \item \textbf{Circularity risk:} LLM-based DR validating LLM-generated edges could introduce systematic bias
    \item \textbf{No expert review:} Enrichment is automated; promoted edges require domain expert validation
    \item \textbf{Preliminary status:} Results are exploratory pending open-source dataset release for community validation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Reproduction:} \texttt{python final\_runs/RQ3\_deep\_research/compute\_enriched\_gt\_metrics.py}

%==============================================================================
\section{Prompt Sensitivity Analysis}
%==============================================================================

To quantify how prompt engineering choices affect performance, we perform sensitivity analysis measuring the variance explained by prompt choice.

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
We treat prompt variant as a categorical factor and test for differences in mean performance using one-way ANOVA on per-run observations. We report the first-order Sobol sensitivity index:
\begin{equation}
S_i = \frac{\text{Var}(\mathbb{E}[Y|X])}{\text{Var}(Y)} = \frac{\text{SS}_\text{between}}{\text{SS}_\text{total}} = \eta^2
\end{equation}
For a single categorical input, $S_i$ equals eta-squared ($\eta^2$) from ANOVA, representing the fraction of output variance explained by prompt choice. Effect sizes are interpreted as: $\eta^2 < 0.01$ negligible, $0.01$--$0.06$ small, $0.06$--$0.14$ medium, $\geq 0.14$ large~\cite{cohen1988statistical}.

\subsection{Input Characterization}
Prompts are embedded using MPNet~\cite{reimers2019sentencebert} (768-dim) and cosine distance from baseline characterizes semantic difference: $d(p_i, p_j) = 1 - \cos(\mathbf{e}_i, \mathbf{e}_j)$. Distances are reported descriptively but not used in the statistical test.

\textbf{Corrector prompts:} Due to cumulative design (500--960 tokens), corrector prompts exceed MPNet's 384-token limit. We use Jina Embeddings v2~\cite{gunther2023jina} (8192-token context) for distance computation only.

\subsection{Output Quantification}
For judges: F1 score (per-run observations across CLDs). For correctors: F1 $\Delta$ = post-correction minus pre-correction F1.

\subsection{Scope}
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Judge (RQ1a):} 4 experiments $\times$ 3--4 prompt variants (Baseline, CoT, Mechanistic, and Mechanistic-Lit for citation)
    \item \textbf{Corrector (RQ1b):} 2 experiments $\times$ 3 prompts (cumulative design)
\end{itemize}

%==============================================================================
\section{Computational Efficiency Analysis}
%==============================================================================

\subsection{Motivation}
Understanding the computational cost of CLD generation and validation is important for evaluating the value proposition of the method compared to human group model building and other automated causal discovery methods. We analyze time and cost scaling to characterize: (1) the relationship between CLD size and computational requirements, (2) the cost-performance trade-off between correctness and citation-based validation, and (3) model efficiency (GPT-4.1 vs. GPT-5-mini).

\subsection{Metrics Extraction}
From each experiment file, we extract from the \texttt{LLM Usage Stats} sheet:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Inference Time (s):} Wall-clock time for LLM API calls
    \item \textbf{Token Counts:} Prompt tokens, completion tokens, and total tokens
    \item \textbf{Number of Edges/Nodes:} From the \texttt{All Edges} sheet
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Cost Estimation}
Estimated costs use OpenAI API pricing (December 2024):
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{GPT-4.1:} \$2.00/1M input, \$8.00/1M output tokens
    \item \textbf{GPT-5-mini:} \$0.15/1M input, \$0.60/1M output tokens (97\% cheaper)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Theoretical Complexity}
Since each edge is judged independently, time complexity is $O(E)$ where $E$ is the number of edges. For dense CLDs where $E \approx N(N-1)$, this implies $O(N^2)$ scaling with respect to nodes. We verify this empirically by fitting linear (time vs. $E$) and quadratic (time vs. $N$) models and reporting $R^2$ values.

\subsection{Parallelization}
The pipeline supports parallel execution at multiple stages:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Edge generation:} Parallel pairwise relationship discovery (\texttt{max\_workers=3--10})
    \item \textbf{Citation filling:} Parallel web search and document retrieval (\texttt{citation\_fill\_max\_workers=5})
    \item \textbf{Judging:} Parallel edge evaluation (\texttt{judge\_max\_workers=10})
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Theoretical speedup:} With $W$ workers and $E$ edges, wall-clock time scales as $O(E/W)$, providing up to $W\times$ speedup. In practice, speedup is limited by: (1) API rate limits (OpenAI: 10K RPM for GPT-4.1), (2) I/O overhead for web search, and (3) load balancing inefficiency for small $E$.

\textbf{Empirical verification:} We benchmark parallelization speedup using 34 API calls (simulating the Depressive CLD) with worker counts of 1, 3, 5, and 10 (3 runs each). Speedup is calculated as $T_{\text{sequential}} / T_{\text{parallel}}$ and efficiency as $\text{Speedup} / W \times 100\%$.

\textbf{Note:} Reported inference times in experiment files represent cumulative API call duration (sum of individual calls), not wall-clock time. Parallelization reduces wall-clock time while maintaining similar cumulative API time.

%==============================================================================
\section{Statistical Assumption Testing and Reproducibility}
\label{sec:assumption_testing}
%==============================================================================

All statistical tests in this thesis include explicit assumption verification. This section documents the tests used and provides commands for reproducibility.

\subsection{ANOVA Assumption Testing (RQ1a, RQ1b, Temperature Sensitivity)}

For all one-way ANOVAs, we verify two key assumptions:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Normality:} Shapiro-Wilk test per group; $p > .05$ indicates normally distributed data
    \item \textbf{Homogeneity of variance:} Levene's test across groups; $p > .05$ indicates equal variances
\end{itemize}

When assumptions are violated but sample sizes are adequate ($n \geq 5$ per group), we report: (1) ANOVA results with caution, and (2) Kruskal-Wallis non-parametric alternative for robustness verification. In all cases where ANOVA was non-significant and assumptions were violated, Kruskal-Wallis confirmed the same conclusion.

\textbf{Reproduction commands:}
\begin{verbatim}
# RQ1a Prompt Sensitivity (ANOVA assumptions)
python3 final_runs/Sensitivity_analysis_simple/scripts/\
    sensitivity_analysis_simple.py --assumptions-only

# RQ1b Corrector Sensitivity (ANOVA assumptions)
python3 final_runs/Sensitivity_analysis_simple/scripts/\
    sensitivity_analysis_corrector.py --assumptions-only

# Temperature Sensitivity (ANOVA assumptions)
python3 final_runs/temperature_sensitivity_assumption_tests.py
\end{verbatim}

Output files are saved to \texttt{final\_runs/*.json} with full test statistics.

\subsection{Proportion Test Assumptions (RQ3)}

For proportion comparisons (TP vs.\ FP detection rates), we verify:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Sample size adequacy:} All expected cell counts $\geq 5$ for chi-square test validity
    \item \textbf{Test selection:} Both two-proportion $z$-test and Fisher's exact test are reported; Fisher's is preferred for robustness
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Reproduction command:}
\begin{verbatim}
python3 final_runs/RQ3_deep_research/rq3_statistical_test.py
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{RQ2 Non-Parametric Methods}

RQ2 uses non-parametric methods that do not require normality assumptions:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Group comparisons:} Mann-Whitney U test (robust to non-normal distributions)
    \item \textbf{Uncertainty quantification:} Bootstrap confidence intervals (10,000 resamples; assumption-free)
    \item \textbf{Correlation:} Pearson $r$ is robust for large $n$ ($>$16,000 edges) via CLT; Spearman's $\rho$ reported for validation
\end{itemize}

See \texttt{data\_science/rq2\_phase*.py} scripts for implementation.

\subsection{Effect Size Reporting}

All significant findings include standardized effect sizes with interpretation following Cohen (1988):
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
    \item \textbf{Cohen's $f$ (ANOVA):} $<$0.10 negligible, 0.10--0.25 small, 0.25--0.40 medium, $\geq$0.40 large
    \item \textbf{Cohen's $d$ (paired differences):} $<$0.20 negligible, 0.20--0.50 small, 0.50--0.80 medium, $\geq$0.80 large
    \item \textbf{Cohen's $h$ (proportions):} Same thresholds as $d$
    \item \textbf{Pearson $r$ (correlation):} $<$0.10 negligible, 0.10--0.30 small, 0.30--0.50 medium, $\geq$0.50 large
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Data Availability:} All analysis scripts and raw data are available in the project repository. See \texttt{final\_runs/} for experiment results and reproducible analysis pipelines.

